 ## 2026-09-03
 
+### 0553 — A doc comment that promises a refusal is only pinnable if the doc is the test's input
+
+- ROOT CAUSE: `Canonicalize`'s doc comment claimed it "rejects malformed UTF-8, invalid surrogate escapes, duplicate object names, and non-I-JSON input". Three of the four were true. The fourth was not: RFC 8785 Section 3.2.2.3 serializes numbers through the ECMAScript `Number.prototype.toString` algorithm on the binary64 value, so `canonical.go` rounded `9007199254740993` to `9007199254740992`, `18446744073709551615` to `18446744073709552000`, and `1.0` to `1`. No live defect — the identity path is gated by `validateAXNumbers` at `canonical.go:199` — but `Canonicalize` is exported, so the first external caller would have inherited a guarantee it does not provide. Audit finding N5, the last of the thirteen second-audit findings.
+- DECISION: option (b), document the rounding as intended, not (a), make it refuse. Both authorities the AC names point the same way. RFC 8785 Appendix B publishes the rounded outputs as *normative test data* and two of its samples are this exact rounding (`0x4340000000000000` → `9007199254740992`, `0x4430000000000000` → `295147905179352830000`), pinned at `canonical_test.go:47-70`; refusing in `Canonicalize` means refusing to be an RFC 8785 canonicalizer and deleting that test. The pinned SPEC never put the refusal there: it puts it on the AX *decoder*, and fixture `NUM-UNSAFE-ROUND` (SPEC.md:302) says "Reject **before identity calculation**". Identity calculation is `CalculateObjectIdentity`/`VerifyObjectIdentity`, and `validateAXNumbers` runs inside `prepareObjectIdentity` before any canonical bytes exist. The implementation already conformed; only the prose lied.
+- FINDING: a doc comment cannot be pinned by a test that *restates* it — that just moves the same unverified claim into a second file where it can drift identically. The pin only works if the comment is the test's **input**. `canonical_number_contract_test.go` parses the `literal -> canonical` rows and the exported entry-point names out of the `Canonicalize` doc with `go/parser` and checks them against real behaviour and against the entry-point set derived from the production call graph. Editing prose without code reddens (M3, M4); editing code without prose reddens (M5). An indented line in the comment that is neither a row nor an exported name is a hard failure, so documented-but-unchecked text cannot accumulate.
+- FINDING: the negative test needed a carrier no other validator would catch. `extensions` is it — `validateExtensionValue` in `closed_shapes.go:1833` accepts any `json.Number` unexamined — so an unsafe integer smuggled through an otherwise valid Session Record can only be refused by `validateAXNumbers`. A green baseline with a safe extension number runs first, or the three refusals would prove nothing.
+- REGRESSION AVOIDED: the first draft derived its own AST call graph recording only `call.Fun.(*ast.Ident)` edges and skipping methods — the identical construction the 0210 entry measured at 3 of 7 UTF-8 guards while it looked complete. Rewritten to reuse `productionCallGraph`/`exportedProductionEntryPoints`/`reachableProductionFunctions` from `utf8_subsumption_test.go`. Shipping a second, weaker graph beside the hardened one would have reopened a blind spot this board had already paid to close, in the same package, three hours later.
+- EVIDENCE: six mutants, each confirmed present in the source before its test was measured. M1 **narrows** rather than deletes — `integer > maxSafeInteger` → `maxSafeInteger+2`, gate still in place, bound moved by two — and the negative test still catches `9007199254740993`. M2 deletes the gate. M3/M4 drift the doc. M5 makes `Canonicalize` call `validateAXNumbers` and the derived set correctly grows to include `CanonicalByteLength`. M6 registers `validateAXNumbers` in a function-value table and routes the gate through it: **behaviour unchanged, `go build` exit 0, negative test still green at exit 0**, and only `TestValidateAXNumbersHasNoCallSiteThisGraphCannotSee` reddens.
+- FINDING: M6 is what separates a stated bound from a held one. The graph resolves a computed callee to every function whose identifier is used as a value in the package, so the derived entry-point set is exact *only while* `validateAXNumbers` is never used as a value. That precondition is not narration — it is the single test standing between a dispatch-table registration and a silently widened set with the whole suite `ok`.
+- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go` (comments only — `Canonicalize`, `CalculateObjectIdentity`, `VerifyObjectIdentity`, `validateAXNumbers`; no logic changed), `internal/canonicaljson/canonical_number_contract_test.go` (new), `README.md`. `canonical_test.go` byte-for-byte unchanged. Package coverage 97.2%, unchanged.
+- ANOMALY: `curator status --check` exits 1 with `worktree: go-testing-tools not-installed`. Pre-existing Story-worktree provisioning state — `.claude/skills/` does not exist in the worktree at all and this diff touches no Curator-managed path. Reported failing, not explained away.
+- STATUS: Ready for review. BUG-260903-3nt3ah. Closes the second adversarial audit's thirteenth and last finding.
